<h2>Three Door Styles. One Finish. One Display.</h2>

<p>5 Blox just commissioned a new showroom display anchored on a single thesis: <strong>White Oak Yosemite Brown</strong> — WW Wood's softly weathered, sun-warmed oak finish — running across three different door styles from two different brands. <a href="/brands/shiloh-cabinetry">Shiloh's</a> <strong>Square Beaded Inset</strong> on one wall, <strong>Flush Inset</strong> in the corner pantry/hutch, and <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry">Eclipse's</a> <strong>Napa</strong> door style on the wine wall and tall pantry. Same oak. Same finish. Three completely different design conversations.</p>

<p>For a homeowner walking through the showroom, that's the kind of side-by-side that resolves a hard question quickly: <em>"Which door style do I actually want?"</em> They don't have to imagine the three options in three different finishes — the finish is constant, the joinery and reveal lines do the talking.</p>

<h2>Why Yosemite Brown</h2>

<p>Yosemite Brown is one of WW Wood's most requested oak finishes for a reason. It reads modern without going pale, warm without going orange, and it lets the rift-cut white oak grain do the work. Pair it with the Sequoia Brown porcelain floor 5 Blox installed and the soft-veined quartzite countertop, and the whole display feels lived-in before anyone has put a single plate on the shelf.</p>

<p>It's also a finish that doesn't fight the architecture. The vaulted ceiling, exposed beams, and woven pendant lights are doing plenty already. The cabinetry settles in underneath instead of competing.</p>

